{
  "summary": "Critiqued the diagnostic meta-agent proposal, identifying several significant weaknesses: (1) The simpler-and-better alternative was overlooked — the core problem is that only the latest review feedback is passed to retries (via `_get_latest_feedback()`), so accumulating all prior feedback into a structured history would capture most of the value without adding a new agent or phase type; (2) Using Haiku for diagnosis is a false economy — analyzing cross-attempt failure patterns and producing actionable prompt modifications is a harder reasoning task than the original work, so a cheap model is likely to produce vague, unhelpful diagnostics that waste the retry rather than saving it; (3) The 'retry with modifications' verdict is underspecified — diagnosing a problem is far easier than prescribing a fix, and the proposal doesn't address how the diagnostic agent would produce concrete, useful prompt modifications vs. generic advice like 'be more specific'; (4) The diagnostic agent itself can fail, creating a meta-retry problem with no proposed solution — do you diagnose the diagnostician?; (5) The proposal introduces a configurable N-per-phase threshold that duplicates the existing max_attempts configuration, adding configuration surface area without clear benefit; (6) The token efficiency concern the previous contributor raised is actually a fundamental tension — compressing failure data for the diagnostic agent loses the detail needed for accurate diagnosis, but passing full outputs blows context windows, and no resolution was proposed; (7) A more incremental approach would deliver most of the value: extend `_get_latest_feedback()` to return a structured summary of ALL prior review feedback (not just the latest), add simple pattern detection inline (e.g., if the same error string appears in 2+ consecutive reviews, flag it in the prompt), and only then consider a full diagnostic agent if the simpler approach proves insufficient."
